A host says each extension whose session_start, or other session event or
resources_discover, took 250 ms or more.

A host's conversation awaits every extension's handlers for an event one
after another, so its session_start costs the sum of every extension's, and
one slow extension holds every open, reload and wake behind it. Plain pi's
own ready lines on a busy machine show 2 to 53 s between loading its
extensions and binding them, but nothing says which extension the time went
to: the runner keeps its extensions private.

Time each extension's handlers for an event, in emit and in
emitResourcesDiscover, and write a line to stderr, which a host's
conversation sends to its journal, for each one at 250 ms or more:
pi-host: "PATH" EVENT took N ms. The path is quoted as JSON, so it stays on
one line. The time is the wall's, awaits included, since that is what an
open waited, so it counts whatever else the thread ran meanwhile. Of what
emit sends, only the session events are said: it also sends a turn's
streamed events, message_update among them, where a slow handler would say
a line a token. The events with dispatch loops of their own (turn_end,
before_agent_start, context, tool_call and the like) are not timed. A line
that can't be written is dropped. Nothing else changes: the handlers
run in the same order, a cancel still returns at once, and an error still
reaches emitError.

Apply only to the host's private pi build; ordinary interactive pi stays
unchanged.

